Prize Categories
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Best Use of Gemma:&lt;/strong&gt; answers, quiz generation and grading, running locally via Ollama&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Best Use of Temporal:&lt;/strong&gt; durable ingestion with retries and resume&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Best Use of Sentry Agent Tracing:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;llm_used&lt;/code&gt; tags and per-stage latency&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-weekend-2026-10-01"&gt;Hacktoberfest Weekend Challenge: Build for a Friend&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My best friend &lt;a href="https://github.com/SanjanaG-01" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;@SanjanaG-01&lt;/a&gt; and I are both taking part in Kestra Hacktober this October. Writing the code is not the hard part. Finding an issue nobody has taken yet is.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On a busy repo, a fresh &lt;code&gt;good first issue&lt;/code&gt; gets its first "can I work on this?" comment within about 20 minutes. By the time you open it, it might already have a pull request, five people asking for it, or a maintainer saying it isn't ready yet. GitHub still shows it as &lt;strong&gt;open&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I measured it. GitHub's own search for open, unassigned good first issues in &lt;code&gt;kestra-io/kestra&lt;/code&gt; returned &lt;strong&gt;31 issues&lt;/strong&gt;. When I checked every one of them:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;21&lt;/strong&gt; already had a pull request (one had three)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;8&lt;/strong&gt; had people asking for them in the comments (up to 11 on a single issue)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;2&lt;/strong&gt; were marked "not ready for contributions"&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;0&lt;/strong&gt; were actually free&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's why I built &lt;strong&gt;Unclaimed&lt;/strong&gt;. Paste a repository, and it tells you which issues are really &lt;strong&gt;Free&lt;/strong&gt;, which are &lt;strong&gt;Claimed&lt;/strong&gt;, which &lt;strong&gt;Have a PR&lt;/strong&gt;, which are &lt;strong&gt;Assigned&lt;/strong&gt; and which are &lt;strong&gt;Not ready&lt;/strong&gt;, with proof for each one. Instead of opening 30 tabs and reading every comment, my friend sees the free ones first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F27yyzssri3bmxb5uyn9u.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F27yyzssri3bmxb5uyn9u.jpg" alt=" " width="799" height="475"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Demo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Try it live: &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://unclaimed-production.up.railway.app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://unclaimed-production.up.railway.app&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Paste any public repo, or a link to a single issue. It works beyond Kestra too: on &lt;code&gt;zed-industries/zed&lt;/code&gt; it found that the repo calls its label &lt;code&gt;.contrib/good first issue&lt;/code&gt;, and showed 4 of the 15 newest issues as free.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fpaqpbxpb1l22zeky5ur1.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fpaqpbxpb1l22zeky5ur1.jpg" alt=" " width="799" height="475"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Code
&lt;/h2&gt;


&lt;div class="ltag-github-readme-tag"&gt;
&lt;div class="readme-overview"&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;
&lt;img src="https://assets.dev.to/assets/github-logo-5a155e1f9a670af7944dd5e12375bc76ed542ea80224905ecaf878b9157cdefc.svg" alt="GitHub logo"&gt;
&lt;a href="https://github.com/Ankith-m1006" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;
Ankith-m1006
&lt;/a&gt; / &lt;a href="https://github.com/Ankith-m1006/unclaimed" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;
unclaimed
&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
Find open-source issues nobody has taken yet. Gemma reads every issue thread for you.
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="ltag-github-body"&gt;

&lt;div id="readme" class="md"&gt;&lt;div class="markdown-heading"&gt;
&lt;h1 class="heading-element"&gt;Unclaimed&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Find open-source issues nobody has taken yet.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;During Hacktoberfest, a fresh &lt;code&gt;good first issue&lt;/code&gt; can get its first "can I work on this?" within 20 minutes. By the time you open it, it may be assigned, already have a pull request, carry a "not ready for contributions" banner, or have five people asking for it in the comments. Unclaimed checks all of that for you.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="markdown-heading"&gt;
&lt;h2 class="heading-element"&gt;How it works&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hard facts from the GitHub API.&lt;/strong&gt; For each open issue with the label you choose, it checks the assignees, linked pull requests (open or merged, from the issue timeline), and the "not ready for contributions" banner some projects add. Any of these settles the verdict without asking the model.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Gemma reads the thread.&lt;/strong&gt; When nothing hard blocks the issue, the comment thread decides. Gemma 3 (4B), an open-weight model, runs on our own Ollama server and reads the issue and its…&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="gh-btn-container"&gt;&lt;a class="gh-btn" href="https://github.com/Ankith-m1006/unclaimed" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;View on GitHub&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;h2&gt;


How I Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Unclaimed works in two steps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Facts from GitHub first.&lt;/strong&gt; For each issue it checks the assignees, the linked pull requests, and the "not ready" banner some projects add. If any of these settles it, the model is not needed at all.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Gemma reads the rest.&lt;/strong&gt; When nothing hard blocks an issue, the comments decide. &lt;strong&gt;Gemma 3 4B&lt;/strong&gt;, running in &lt;strong&gt;Ollama&lt;/strong&gt; on our own small server, reads the issue and its latest comments and returns structured JSON: who asked to be assigned, who says they're working on it, what a maintainer decided, a one-line summary of the work, and a difficulty estimate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every claim comes with its proof: the person's real comment, their avatar, the date, and a link. You don't have to trust the model blindly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F3srmrdosqy3f9z0jd9sg.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F3srmrdosqy3f9z0jd9sg.jpg" alt=" " width="799" height="475"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The stack is deliberately small: plain Node.js, one HTML page, Gemma 3 through Ollama, and two containers on Railway.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What went wrong (and how I fixed it):&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The 1B model wasn't good enough.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;gemma3:1b&lt;/code&gt; was fast, but it said "nobody asked" on an issue where five people had. Switching to &lt;code&gt;gemma3:4b&lt;/code&gt; fixed accuracy at about 6 to 16 seconds per issue on CPU.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Requests timing out.&lt;/strong&gt; The small model kept generating inside the JSON until it hit the timeout. Capping the answer length fixed it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One rule made it worse.&lt;/strong&gt; Someone wrote "I think you missed assigning this to Omer", and it counted as a claim. When I told Gemma to only count people asking for themselves, it became too cautious and missed all five real claimants. A small code rule worked better: let the model be generous, then correct it with cheap checks you can verify.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hidden maintainers.&lt;/strong&gt; On freeCodeCamp, a maintainer's standard note was counted as six claims, because many maintainers keep their organisation membership private. Now anyone who labelled or assigned the issue counts as a maintainer.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The token that hid pull requests.&lt;/strong&gt; After I added a read-only GitHub token, issues with open PRs suddenly looked free. GitHub leaves pull request links out of the issue history for fine-grained tokens. The fix was to also search for PRs that mention the issue.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I checked it against 8 real issues I had read by hand, and it got all 8 right.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Does Open Innovation Matter?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Unclaimed reads other people's comments and usernames. With an open model on our own server, none of that goes to a closed AI company. There's no per-request bill either, so my friend can re-check as often as she likes during a race that is measured in minutes. And because it's open, she can run the whole thing on her own laptop with &lt;code&gt;ollama pull gemma3:4b&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;node server.js&lt;/code&gt;. An open model helping people make their first open-source contribution felt like the right fit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Prize Categories
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Best Use of Gemma&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;Built with my best friend &lt;a href="https://github.com/SanjanaG-01" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;@SanjanaG-01&lt;/a&gt;. Good luck to everyone in Hacktober, and read the thread before you ask for an issue!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Thank you&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fntukc1lfthvfckmnni2f.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fntukc1lfthvfckmnni2f.png" alt=" " width="583" height="195"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://postiz.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Postiz&lt;/a&gt; Es una herramienta con licencia &lt;code&gt;GNU Affero General Public License v3.0&lt;/code&gt; a día de hoy (04/10/2026) y como soy un gran defensor del software libre quería trastear con ella.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Motivos para trastear con ella, es una herramienta que permite configurar y programar publicaciones en diferentes redes sociales.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tiene una API para que desde un CMS puedas pasarle lo que se quiere publicar y luego ella lo publica.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Se puede tener levantada en un contenedor o pagar como SaaS según lo que prefieras, en mi caso tengo un equipo viejo en casa que quiero usar para tener este tipo de herramientas.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-weekend-2026-10-01"&gt;Hacktoberfest Weekend Challenge: Build for a Friend&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


I Built an AI Judge That Doesn't Decide Who Wins
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My friend participates in hackathons frequently and builds strong projects, but she had an important concern:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How can you know that judges are applying the same rubric consistently?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A project can receive different scores from different judges even when everyone is evaluating the same criteria.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I built &lt;strong&gt;FairJudge AI&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;FairJudge is an AI-assisted hackathon evaluation system designed to make judging more &lt;strong&gt;transparent, evidence-based, and consistent&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It can:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Evaluate projects against a supplied rubric&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Connect scores to supporting evidence&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Identify missing evidence&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Explain why a score was given&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Compare multiple judges&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Detect significant scoring disagreements&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Recommend human review when judges disagree&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But there is one important thing FairJudge &lt;strong&gt;doesn't&lt;/strong&gt; do:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;It does not decide who the objectively correct winner is.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The final decision always remains with human judges.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


The Problem
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hackathon judging involves multiple judges evaluating projects across several criteria.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Disagreements can happen because:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Judges interpret criteria differently&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Evidence may be incomplete&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Some claims cannot be verified&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Judges may emphasize different aspects of the same project&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of building another AI that simply says:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;"Project X should win."&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted to build something more transparent:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why was this score given? What evidence supports it? What evidence is missing? Where do judges disagree?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That became &lt;strong&gt;FairJudge AI&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Demo
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;🎥 &lt;strong&gt;Demo Video:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://claude.ai/artifact/9JLZQKa7C33ADuJg2Qk5wC" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://claude.ai/artifact/9JLZQKa7C33ADuJg2Qk5wC&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The demo shows:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Evaluating a hackathon project&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Analyzing supporting and missing evidence&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Generating an AI-assisted evaluation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Comparing multiple judges&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Detecting significant disagreements&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Rejecting unsupported evidence&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Recommending human review&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


How FairJudge Works
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The core workflow is:&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;
text
Project + Rubric
↓
Evidence-based AI Evaluation
↓
Evidence Validation
↓
Deterministic Python Scoring
↓
Multiple Judge Comparison
↓
Disagreement Detection
↓
Human Review
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Attackers began exploiting CVE-2026-61500, a critical flaw in the Rejetto HFS file server, on October 3, 2026. That was one day after Horizon3.ai published how it works, &lt;a href="https://www.theregister.com/security/2026/10/03/anthropics-super-bug-hunting-model-mythos-is-hardcore-good-at-math-as-latest-vuln-under-attack-shows/5300933" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The Register&lt;/a&gt; reports. The bug lets anyone forge an administrator login and then run code on the server. It was found with Mythos, Anthropic's vulnerability-hunting AI model, and HFS 3.2.1 fixes it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What Rejetto HFS is and which versions are affected
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;HFS, short for HTTP File Server, is a free, open-source program that shares files from a computer through a web browser. Versions 3.0.0 through 3.2.0 are vulnerable, according to &lt;a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/rejetto-hfs-session-forgery-via-predictable-signing-key" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;VulnCheck's advisory&lt;/a&gt;. Version 3.2.1 is the first fixed release.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The two main scores disagree slightly. VulnCheck rates the flaw 9.3 out of 10, while the Strix CVE database lists 9.8. Both put it in the critical band.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Detail&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Value&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Affected versions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;HFS 3.0.0 through 3.2.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Fixed version&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;HFS 3.2.1&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Severity score&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;9.3 (VulnCheck) or 9.8 (Strix)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;CVE published&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;July 13, 2026&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Exploit details published&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;October 2, 2026&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;First attacks seen&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;October 3, 2026&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How the attack works
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The root cause is a random number generator that is not built for secrets. HFS creates the key that signs its login cookies with JavaScript's &lt;code&gt;Math.random()&lt;/code&gt;. VulnCheck says HFS also "discloses outputs of the same generator to unauthenticated clients during login."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That pairing is the problem. In Google's V8 engine, which runs Node.js, &lt;code&gt;Math.random()&lt;/code&gt; uses an algorithm called xorshift128+. It is fast but fully predictable. Anyone who sees enough of its outputs can work out its internal state, and then every number it will produce.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An attacker therefore starts login attempts and collects the leaked numbers. The Register says Horizon3 then used Z3, a tool that solves equations automatically, to rebuild the generator's state. From there, the attacker recovers the signing key and forges an administrator cookie.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"If an attacker can derive what the session signing key is, they can forge valid session cookies," Horizon3 researcher Zach Hanley told The Register. Admin access then leads to code execution through HFS's own &lt;code&gt;server_code&lt;/code&gt; setting, VulnCheck says.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What Mythos found that a scanner might miss
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hanley credits Mythos with spotting the chain, not just the weak generator. Mythos noticed the insecure generator and the separate code path that leaked its outputs. It "recognized those two facts as a chain," Hanley told The Register. A tool that flags only &lt;code&gt;Math.random()&lt;/code&gt; would report a weakness without proving it could be exploited.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mythos is the invitation-only model Anthropic &lt;a href="https://techaiwire.com/articles/claude-fable-5-1-mythos-5-1-cache-price-cut/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;updated to Mythos 5.1 in September&lt;/a&gt;. It is credited with 286 CVEs as of October 3, The Register reports. The Register also counts this as the second Anthropic-linked flaw exploited in the wild since Horizon3 joined Project Glasswing, Anthropic's security program, in July 2026. VulnCheck credits the discovery to Hanley working with Anthropic Research.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Who is attacking
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;VulnCheck saw the first attacks on October 3 against servers in the US and Japan, coming from addresses hosted in China, The Register reports. Later attempts came through proxy servers in the US.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The timing shows how little a fix does on its own. The CVE record, which already pointed to the 3.2.1 fix, dates back to July 13. Yet at publication, the EPSS model, which estimates how likely a flaw is to be exploited within 30 days, put the odds at 0.75%, both VulnCheck and Strix say. The first attacks VulnCheck saw came only after a step-by-step write-up and a video existed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What this means for developers
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you run HFS, upgrade to 3.2.1 now. Any server on 3.0.0 to 3.2.0 that is reachable from the internet should be treated as possibly compromised. Check its admin settings, especially &lt;code&gt;server_code&lt;/code&gt;, for anything you did not add.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The wider lesson is for anyone writing JavaScript. Never use &lt;code&gt;Math.random()&lt;/code&gt; for keys, tokens, session IDs or password resets. In Node.js, use &lt;code&gt;crypto.randomBytes()&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;crypto.randomUUID()&lt;/code&gt;. In browsers, use &lt;code&gt;crypto.getRandomValues()&lt;/code&gt;. Then search your own code for &lt;code&gt;Math.random(&lt;/code&gt; near words like key, token, secret or session.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Also check what your app leaks. HFS was exploitable because it sent raw generator outputs to visitors who had not logged in. Random-looking IDs in error pages, login responses or headers can feed the same kind of attack.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Finally, do not wait for the exploit to patch. A low EPSS score measures attacker interest before a public write-up, not after one. AI tools like Mythos are now finding and explaining bugs faster, so the gap between a write-up and the first attack is likely to stay short.&lt;/p&gt;




